WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:09.580 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  dr. Edwards Deming said if you can't  describe work as a process you can't  improve it one of the most powerful and  accessible process improvement tools we  have to learn with is something called a  process map a process map is a visual  display of the activities of a process  or a system process maps are extremely  useful and helpful for understanding how  a current process is designed by  enabling you to quickly identify  opportunities for improvement once a  process has been improved process Maps  can also act as guidance to support  people doing standard work and following  a designed process reliably process  mapping is a powerful exercise for  learning and improving I often describe  it as one of the secret weapons for  helping teams to identify variation and  potential failure points in their work  processes in an ideal scenario I like to  do process mapping with a mixed group of  stakeholders of the process we're trying  to improve I also strive to go to where  work happens versus doing it in a  conference room or a meeting space I  begin by inviting the team to think  about the average patient or someone who  represents the normal population this  process must serve every day then I ask  them to attempt to describe the process  at a macro level as high level as  possible so let's take an example here  and draw just a simple diagram so  usually every process has a start and  the start in the end often use a shape  that looks like a pill and then what you  do is you start to ask yourself what are  each of the steps in the process and so  we might go and say well here's step one  the box there and step two and step  three and and maybe after step three  there's actually a decision that has to  be made so we reflect that by making it  a diamond and that may bring us to an  extra process step down here so if yes  for example it may continue on I'll put  a little Y here or just right yes it'll  continue on  down the process if it's no then there  might be a decision to go down here and  fix something before the process  continues and then we keep going until  we reach the end and again at the end we  often describe it as a pill so each step  along the way right we've got process  step one and two and three and then  there was a decision that had to be made  and so that either takes us to step four  or it drops us down here to this step  before we move up to four but it's  basically a simple visual display of the  steps in a process from start to finish  now in my experience most teams won't  make it past the first step or the  second step in a process before they  start describing multiple ways a step  can be done or they just disagree on  what the step is or identify a failure  point all of this is fantastic for  generating improvement ideas this  dialogue and listening here is essential  for learning and I try to balance  keeping people focused on getting to a  first draft process and honoring and  capturing all of the input from team  members there are a number of different  approaches you can take in doing a  process map from the very basic  hand-drawn flow diagram on a whiteboard  or a piece of paper like I just did to  more complex variations including value  stream analysis spaghetti diagrams  swimlane diagrams each helps us  understand better how work actually  happens process mapping is a great tool  think about a process you want to better  understand and see if you can map it out  from start to finish  you

